  {
   "id": "nep-deep-field",
   "title": "North ecliptic pole deep field",
   "description": "SPHEREx flies over the Earth's poles, so on every orbit it looks at the same two spots near the ecliptic poles. This patch near the north ecliptic pole has been photographed about 20000 times in DR1. These nine visits, from May 2025 to January 2026, were all taken at nearly the same colour (0.88 to 0.92 microns).",
   "look_for": "Flip through the frames: the stars and the small oval galaxy up and right of the centre stay put, because they are fixed in the sky. The black edges move because SPHEREx was turned to a different angle on each visit (the app lines the frames up for you). Anything that appears in only one frame, like a short streak, is a satellite or a cosmic ray hit.",
