  {
   "id": "cats-eye-spectrum",
   "title": "Cat's Eye Nebula: a spectrum from many visits",
   "description": "NGC 6543, the Cat's Eye, is the glowing gas shell thrown off by a dying star. It sits inside the SPHEREx deep field near the north ecliptic pole, which SPHEREx observes thousands of times. Every visit lands on a slightly different wavelength, so adding the visits up builds a spectrum from 0.75 to 5 microns.",
   "look_for": "The spectrum is fairly flat with one big spike near 1.85 microns. That spike is hydrogen gas glowing (the Paschen alpha line), the same kind of light that makes nebulae shine red in visible photos.",
